ct story title on first citation; (2) the Rubin encounter passage misreads the metaphorical quote \"That combination stayed with me\" as a claim that Rubin physically stayed on after his residency, and then mischaracterizes what struck him â€” substituting \"Odeh's work oriented toward peace rather than animosity\" for the source's actual dual observation of repeated vandalism alongside public invisibility. The frontmatter summary inherits both paragraph-one errors and carries a garbled trailing artifact; repair_editorial_surfaces will regenerate it from the corrected body. The structure, attribution consistency, date accuracy, and modality on the unsolved murder are all sound.","requiredRepairs":["Paragraph 1 â€” overstatement: 'the most prominent public tribute to one of Arab America's most noted civil rights figures' must be corrected to reflect the source's actual claim ('one of the few monuments honoring an Arab-American in the United States'), not a superlative the source does not assert.","Paragraph 1 â€” missing sculpture element: the manuscript says 'a dove resting in his outstretched hand' but the source states Odeh is depicted 'holding a book in one hand and a dove in the other.' The book is a material detail of the artwork and must be restored.","Paragraph 1 â€” citation form: writing law requires the first inline link to use the exact story title as link text. '[Hyperallergic](URL)' must become '[A Living Memorial to Palestinian Activist Alex Odeh Takes Flight](URL)'.","Rubin paragraph â€” misread of source quote: 'was moved enough to stay on' renders the metaphorical 'That combination stayed with me' as a claim about physical persistence at the Santa Ana location, which the source does not state.","Rubin paragraph â€” mischaracterization of Rubin's observation: 'it struck him how thoroughly Odeh's work had been oriented toward peace rather than animosity' substitutes the writer's gloss for the source's actual account, which is that Rubin was struck by the contrast between the memorial drawing repeated vandalism AND being largely unknown to the surrounding public â€” a paired observation, not a reflection on Odeh's personal orientation.","Frontmatter summary: inherits the 'most prominent' overstatement and the missing book, and ends with a garbled ', the same source reports.' fragment. Apply repair_editorial_surfaces after body corrections."],"repairTools":[{"tool":"replace_exact_body_text","before":"Outside the public library in Santa Ana, California, a decades-old bronze sculpture has served as the most prominent public tribute to one of Arab America's most noted civil rights figures. Commissioned in 1994 from Khalil Bendib, an artist of Algerian-American background, the work portrays Palestinian-American activist Alex Odeh with a dove resting in his outstretched hand, according to [Hyperallergic](https://hyperallergic.com/a-living-memorial-to-palestinian-activist-alex-odeh-takes-flight/).","after":"Outside the public library in Santa Ana, California, a decades-old bronze sculpture stands as one of the few public monuments in the United States honoring an Arab-American â€” a memorial to one of the community's most noted civil rights figures. Commissioned in 1994 from Algerian-American artist Khalil Bendib, the work portrays Palestinian-American activist Alex Odeh holding a book in one hand and a dove in the other, symbolizing his work as a peace activist, according to [A Living Memorial to Palestinian Activist Alex Odeh Takes Flight](https://hyperallergic.com/a-living-memorial-to-palestinian-activist-alex-odeh-takes-flight/)."},{"tool":"replace_exact_body_text","before":"and was moved enough to stay on, according to Hyperallergic. Rubin told the outlet that many people he encountered â€” among them a librarian at the site â€” had no prior knowledge of Odeh, and that it struck him how thoroughly Odeh's work had been oriented toward peace rather than animosity.","after":"according to Hyperallergic. He was struck by a dual contrast: the monument drew repeated acts of vandalism, yet many people nearby â€” among them a librarian â€” were entirely unfamiliar with Odeh's life and work. \"That combination stayed with me,\" Rubin told Hyperallergic: a memorial simultaneously attacked and overlooked."},{"tool":"repair_editorial_surfaces"}],"report":"## Editorial Assessment â€” reporting-source-1-for-living-memorial-palestinian-activist-alex\n\n### Source audit\n\nThe single admitted source is the Hyperallergic article published 2026-07-17, witnessed in full via the research packet summary. That summary is rich and specific: dates, names, direct quotes, event sequence, and the full description of the sculpture. The manuscript draws on it fairly but commits five material errors against it.\n\n### Material defects found\n\n**1. Overstatement of the monument's status (paragraph 1)**\nManuscript: \"the most prominent public tribute to one of Arab America's most noted civil rights figures.\"\nSource: \"One of the few monuments honoring an Arab-American in the United States.\"\nThese are categorically different claims. \"One of the few\" is a scarcity assertion; \"the most prominent\" is a superlative ranking the source does not make. The error strengthens source claim to newsroom fact in violation of headline law applied to body copy.\n\n**2. Missing sculpture element â€” the book (paragraph 1)**\nManuscript: \"Alex Odeh with a dove resting in his outstretched hand.\"\nSource: \"depicts Odeh holding a book in one hand and a dove in the other, symbolizing his work as a peace activist.\"\nThe book is not decorative detail â€” it is a co-equal symbol in a dual-symbol sculpture. Dropping it misrepresents the artwork that is the article's central subject.\n\n**3. Citation form on first use (paragraph 1)**\nThe writing law is explicit: \"cite THE STORY as [Exact Story Title](its-exact-url) before making its first factual claim.\" The manuscript's first inline link reads \"[Hyperallergic](URL)\" rather than \"[A Living Memorial to Palestinian Activist Alex Odeh Takes Flight](URL).\" The URL is correct; only the link text is non-compliant. This is a pipeline-binding form requirement, not a judgment call.\n\n**4. Misread of direct quote â€” \"stay on\" (Rubin paragraph)**\nManuscript: \"was moved enough to stay on, according to Hyperallergic.\"\nSource: \"'That combination stayed with me,' Rubin told Hyperallergic.\"\nThe source quote is a statement about a mental or emotional impression persisting â€” not a claim that Rubin physically remained in Santa Ana after his residency. The manuscript turns an interior metaphor into a behavioral fact the source does not assert.\n\n**5. Mischaracterization of Rubin's observation (Rubin paragraph)**\nManuscript: \"it struck him how thoroughly Odeh's work had been oriented toward peace rather than animosity.\"\nSource: Rubin was struck by the monument being \"simultaneously being attacked and overlooked: a work that remained politically charged enough to attract repeated acts of vandalism, yet had become somewhat invisible to the public around it.\"\nThe source identifies a dual contrast â€” vandalism alongside anonymity. The manuscript collapses this into a characterization of Odeh's personal orientation, which is a different and unsupported claim. Rubin's observation is about the monument's paradoxical reception, not about Odeh's documented commitments.\n\n### What holds up\n\nThe remaining factual core is solid. Odeh's birthplace (Jifna, 1944), the Nakba, the 1967 occupation, the 1972 immigration and Cal State Fullerton degree, the ADC directorship, the inter-community dialogue work, the October 11 1985 pipe bomb, the JDL allegation with preserved modality (\"identifiedâ€¦ as possibly responsible\"), the officially unsolved status, the vandalism dates (1996, 1997, 2020 dove theft), the 2023 GCAC residency, the Sundance debut, the December removal â€” all check cleanly against the witnessed source. The article's attribution is consistent throughout and the headline does not overstate.\n\n### Frontmatter surfaces\n\nThe summary in frontmatter carries the \"most prominent\" error, omits the book from the sculpture description, and terminates with a garbled fragment (\", the same source reports.\"). Because Koios's deterministic adapter projects the summary from the admitted body, repair_editorial_surfaces after body correction will regenerate these surfaces cleanly.\n\n### Demand-lens h2 warning\n\nThe deterministic standards gate flagged `demand-lens-missing-h2` as a warning. At 529 words within the 300â€“650 brief commission, the absence of section headings is consistent with brief format and does not independently block publication. The warning is noted but does not add to required repairs.\n\n### Pipeline diagnosis\n\nThe sources are sufficient and the assignment is legitimate. The Hyperallergic article is specific, named, and fully witnessed. The errors are all traceable to the writer model misreading the source â€” overstating a quantitative qualifier, omitting a named artwork element, misinterpreting a direct metaphorical quote as a behavioral claim, and substituting a thematic gloss for the source's actual paired observation. These are writer-model failures, not source or assignment failures. All five errors are local and correctable via bounded replace_exact_body_text edits without structural replay."}ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;TMbYrQdwXSiR-MlTnxu0joLqUkNF9_ytu96pnHPi3Pwƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx$8e3a09d6-91f5-4195-aafc-11be2bb9ab24ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;Ljo6446b1FE2OmEqOVbvYIp2TdfRrsX0k0cEdBvoWgYƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkhtool_useƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;K07gDHbYijmWr5fdCVgKSLyRb2RzSbEW4y5ZNq_8CA4ƒdpairƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcs